UNIT isRegExp
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG isRegExp(value: any) -> boolean
DEPENDS-ON none   # this build inlines every dependency; zero external deps

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EXPORT SHAPE  (mandatory for the blind re-emitter)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The module's DEFAULT export IS the predicate function. There is exactly one
# export and it is value-mode:
#     module.exports = isRegExp            (the original, CommonJS)
#     export default <fn>                  (what the blind re-emitter MUST emit)
# The re-emitter ships the FUNCTION VALUE as the ES-module default export. The
# oracle calls it as `fn(value)` with a single positional argument.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BEHAVIOR  (ordered, exact, complete)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# isRegExp(value) returns a boolean: true IFF `value` is a genuine RegExp
# object, else false. Define it via the package's FALLBACK implementation
# `baseIsRegExp`, which is the seam-free, portable, observationally-correct
# definition for every in-envelope input (see SEAM ANALYSIS below):
#
#   regexpTag       = '[object RegExp]'
#   objectToString  = Object.prototype.toString   # the ORIGINAL, unpatched
#
#   isObject(value):
#       type = typeof value
#       return  (!!value)  AND  (type == 'object'  OR  type == 'function')
#       # NOTE: !!value rules out null and the falsy primitives; 'object'
#       #       and 'function' are the only object-language typeofs.
#
#   baseIsRegExp(value):
#       return  isObject(value)  AND  objectToString.call(value) == regexpTag
#
#   isRegExp(value):
#       return baseIsRegExp(value)
#
# Pipeline, in order, for an arbitrary value:
#   NODE-1  Compute t = typeof value.
#   NODE-2  Guard: if value is falsy (null, undefined, 0, -0, NaN, '', false,
#           0n) -> return false immediately (the `!!value` term short-circuits;
#           in particular null/undefined are false here, NOT via any tag path).
#   NODE-3  Guard: if t is neither 'object' nor 'function' -> return false.
#           (Catches: string, number, boolean, bigint, symbol — all primitive
#           typeofs that are not object/function.)
#   NODE-4  Tag check: return Object.prototype.toString.call(value) ===
#           '[object RegExp]'.
#           - A genuine RegExp (literal /x/, new RegExp(...), RegExp subclass
#             instance, cross-realm RegExp) has internal tag '[object RegExp]'
#             -> true.
#           - Everything else object/function-typed (plain object, array, Set,
#             Map, Uint8Array, Buffer/Node buffers, Date, Error, boxed
#             Number/String/Boolean, any function) has a different tag
#             -> false.
#
# Determinism / purity: pure, total, no throw on any input (every branch
# returns a boolean), no I/O, no mutation of `value`, no global state read
# OTHER than the seam discussed below. Idempotent. Read-only (no in-place
# mutation concern; N/A).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SEAM ANALYSIS  (why ORACLE-CLASS is deterministic, NOT non-deterministic)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The ORIGINAL source selects its implementation at load time:
#
#     nodeIsRegExp = (process.binding('util') || {}).isRegExp   # may be absent
#     isRegExp = nodeIsRegExp ? baseUnary(nodeIsRegExp) : baseIsRegExp
#
# So the LIVE unit may dispatch to Node's native `util.isRegExp` (when
# process.binding('util') is reachable) OR to baseIsRegExp (otherwise). This is
# an ambient ENV/host seam (presence of a deprecated Node internal binding).
#
# It is NOT a quarantine seam because the two implementations are
# OBSERVATIONALLY EQUIVALENT over the ENTIRE input envelope this oracle can
# produce. VERIFIED EMPIRICALLY (node v24.16.0, native path active):
#   - For every codec-faithful value class (RegExp of any flags, string,
#     number, -0, NaN, +/-Infinity, boolean, bigint, null, undefined, plain
#     object, array, Set, Map, Uint8Array, Buffer), native(value) === base(value).
#   - The ONLY input where they diverge is an ordinary object carrying a forged
#     `Symbol.toStringTag === 'RegExp'` property:
#         base   -> true   (Object.prototype.toString yields '[object RegExp]')
#         native -> false  (real structural check)
#     That input is OUT-OF-ENVELOPE: a Symbol-keyed property does NOT survive
#     the factory codec (JSON.stringify drops it; the round-tripped object is a
#     plain {} -> both paths agree, false). The generator NEVER emits it.
#
# Therefore the seam is invisible to the oracle: the blind re-emitter
# implements the seam-free `baseIsRegExp` definition above and converges with
# the live package on every gradable input. The deployed wrapper may use the
# native fast path at runtime; the verified core is the equivalent fallback.
# No injected collaborator is required. ORACLE-CLASS deterministic.

ORACLE value -> oracle.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SCOPE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly):
#   - isObject gate (!!value && (typeof in {'object','function'})).
#   - Object.prototype.toString.call(value) === '[object RegExp]' tag check.
#   - The TRUE set within the envelope: RegExp instances of any flag
#     combination and any source (/x/, /x/g, /x/gimsuy, new RegExp('ab','i'),
#     new RegExp('')). The codec stores a RegExp by (source, flags) and rebuilds
#     a genuine RegExp -> tag '[object RegExp]' -> true.
#   - The FALSE set within the envelope (each demonstrated in genInputs):
#       string (incl. '/x/'), number (incl. 0, -0, NaN, +/-Infinity), boolean,
#       bigint, null, undefined, plain object {}, populated object, array,
#       empty array, Set, Map, Uint8Array, Buffer.
#       Falsy primitives short-circuit at NODE-2; non-falsy non-object/function
#       primitives fail at NODE-3; object-typed non-regexps fail at NODE-4.
#
# CARRIED DATA: none. (No literal tables. Two constants only: the string
#   '[object RegExp]' and the typeof tags 'object'/'function' — given verbatim
#   in BEHAVIOR above, not a table.)
#
# OUT-OF-CONTRACT / DOCUMENT-AS-OUT (NOT emitted by genInputs; a blind
# re-emitter still reproduces them from the spec, but the codec cannot carry
# them, so they are not gradable held-out inputs):
#   - Functions and arrow functions: isObject is true (typeof 'function') but
#     tag is '[object Function]' (or '[object AsyncFunction]', etc.) != regexpTag
#     -> FALSE. Not codec-round-trippable.
#   - Symbols: typeof 'symbol' is neither object nor function -> NODE-3 -> FALSE.
#     Not codec-round-trippable.
#   - boxed primitives (new Number/String/Boolean): isObject true, tag is
#     '[object Number|String|Boolean]' != regexpTag -> FALSE. Boxed primitives
#     do not round-trip the codec, so excluded from genInputs (but a blind
#     re-emitter would still compute FALSE for them).
#   - Object with forged Symbol.toStringTag === 'RegExp': the ONLY native-vs-
#     fallback divergence; out-of-envelope (see SEAM ANALYSIS). Not emitted.
#   - process.binding('util') host seam itself: an ambient ENV dependency,
#     resolved at load time, observationally equivalent over the envelope; not a
#     runtime input, not quarantined.

